.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt RAND_BYTES 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm RAND_bytes ,
.Nm RAND_priv_bytes ,
.Nm RAND_bytes_ex ,
.Nm RAND_priv_bytes_ex ,
.Nm RAND_pseudo_bytes ,
.Nm RAND_set1_random_provider
.Nd generate random data
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/rand.h
.Ft int
.Fo RAND_bytes
.Fa "unsigned char *buf"
.Fa "int num"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo RAND_priv_bytes
.Fa "unsigned char *buf"
.Fa "int num"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo RAND_bytes_ex
.Fa "OSSL_LIB_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "unsigned char *buf"
.Fa "size_t num"
.Fa "unsigned int strength"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo RAND_priv_bytes_ex
.Fa "OSSL_LIB_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "unsigned char *buf"
.Fa "size_t num"
.Fa "unsigned int strength"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo RAND_set1_random_provider
.Fa "OSSL_LIB_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "OSSL_PROVIDER *p"
.Fc
The following function has been deprecated since OpenSSL 1.1.0, and can
be hidden entirely by defining
.Ar OPENSSL_API_COMPAT
with a suitable version value, see
.Xr openssl_user_macros 7 :
.Ft int
.Fo RAND_pseudo_bytes
.Fa "unsigned char *buf"
.Fa "int num"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn RAND_bytes
generates
.Fa num
random bytes using a cryptographically secure pseudo random generator
(CSPRNG) and stores them in
.Fa buf .
.Fa buf
.Sy MUST NOT
be NULL.
.Pp
.Fn RAND_priv_bytes
has the same semantics as
.Fn RAND_bytes .
It is intended to be used for generating values that should remain
private.
If using the default RAND_METHOD, this function uses a separate
"private" PRNG instance so that a compromise of the "public" PRNG
instance will not affect the secrecy of these private values, as
described in
.Xr RAND 7
and
.Xr EVP_RAND 7 .
.Pp
.Fn RAND_bytes_ex
and
.Fn RAND_priv_bytes_ex
are the same as
.Fn RAND_bytes
and
.Fn RAND_priv_bytes
except that they both take additional
.Fa strength
and
.Fa ctx
parameters.
The bytes generated will have a security strength of at least
.Fa strength
bits.
The DRBG used for the operation is the public or private DRBG associated
with the specified
.Fa ctx .
The parameter can be NULL, in which case the default library context is
used (see
.Xr OSSL_LIB_CTX 3 .
If the default RAND_METHOD has been changed then for compatibility
reasons the RAND_METHOD will be used in preference and the DRBG of the
library context ignored.
.Pp
.Fn RAND_set1_random_provider
specifies a provider,
.Em prov ,
which will be used by the library context
.Fa ctx
for all of the generate calls above instead of the built-in in DRBGs and
entropy source.
Pass NULL for the provider to disable the random provider functionality.
In this case, the built-in DRBGs and entropy source will be used.
This call should not be considered thread safe.
.Sh NOTES
By default, the OpenSSL CSPRNG supports a security level of 256 bits,
provided it was able to seed itself from a trusted entropy source.
On all major platforms supported by OpenSSL (including the Unix-like
platforms and Windows), OpenSSL is configured to automatically seed the
CSPRNG on first use using the operating systems's random generator.
.Pp
If the entropy source fails or is not available, the CSPRNG will enter
an error state and refuse to generate random bytes.
For that reason, it is important to always check the error return value
of
.Fn RAND_bytes
and
.Fn RAND_priv_bytes
and not take randomness for granted.
.Pp
On other platforms, there might not be a trusted entropy source
available or OpenSSL might have been explicitly configured to use
different entropy sources.
If you are in doubt about the quality of the entropy source, don't
hesitate to ask your operating system vendor or post a question on
GitHub or the openssl-users mailing list.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn RAND_bytes
and
.Fn RAND_priv_bytes
return 1 on success, -1 if not supported by the current RAND method, or
0 on other failure.
The error code can be obtained by
.Xr ERR_get_error 3 .
.Pp
.Fn RAND_set1_random_provider
returns 1 on success and 0 on failure.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr RAND_add 3 ,
.Xr RAND_bytes 3 ,
.Xr RAND_priv_bytes 3 ,
.Xr ERR_get_error 3 ,
.Xr RAND 7 ,
.Xr EVP_RAND 7
.Sh HISTORY
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Fn RAND_pseudo_bytes
was deprecated in OpenSSL 1.1.0; use
.Fn RAND_bytes
instead.
.It
The
.Fn RAND_priv_bytes
function was added in OpenSSL 1.1.1.
.It
The
.Fn RAND_bytes_ex
and
.Fn RAND_priv_bytes_ex
functions were added in OpenSSL 3.0
.It
The
.Fn RAND_set1_random_provider
function was added in OpenSSL 3.5
.El
